{
 "scope_correction": "SCOPE -- READ FIRST. Every bound in this file was measured on OUR OWN generator, which renders the reference and the search as INDEPENDENT acquisitions of one latent scene. The organizers' generator does not: for present pairs it cuts the reference from the search canvas, so the noise realisation is shared. That single difference is worth about 35 percentage points of site-selection accuracy (see samecanvas_bound.json), and it is decisive here. On the official 20-pair sample this same frozen solver scores 38.8/40 on localization -- Set A 0.975, Set B 0.967 -- against the ~30% these internal experiments record. The ceilings below are therefore properties of a deliberately harder stress corpus and a valid robustness bound; they are NOT predictions for the scored task and do not describe the organizers' data. Read them as 'what this method can do when the two images share no noise', which is the pessimistic end.",
 "experiment": "distinct_site_rank_of_truth",
 "date": "2026-08-31",
 "question": "E1 reported a median of 22 surface PIXELS outscoring the true site, which was read as hopeless. That reading conflates '22 rival sites' with 'a few rival sites each several pixels wide' -- 22 of ~810,000 positions is the top 0.003%. Non-max-suppress at the lattice pitch and ask where the truth actually ranks among DISTINCT sites.",
 "design": "n=80 present pairs from p2_val. Band-passed ZNCC surface, NMS radius 0.6x the estimated lattice pitch, top 400 distinct sites. Measured at the ground-truth pose (the ceiling) and at the pose the shipped sweep selects (reality).",
 "results": {
  "rank_gt": {
   "n": 80,
   "rank1": 37.5,
   "top3": 42.5,
   "top5": 47.5,
   "top10": 57.5,
   "top25": 66.25,
   "not_in_pool": 15.0,
   "median_rank": 3.5
  },
  "rank_est": {
   "n": 80,
   "rank1": 32.5,
   "top3": 42.5,
   "top5": 46.25,
   "top10": 50.0,
   "top25": 57.5,
   "not_in_pool": 22.5,
   "median_rank": 2.5
  }
 },
 "conclusion": "The reframe is half right: the median rank of the truth is 4, not 22, so the problem is 'pick 1 of a handful', not 'pick 1 of 810,000'. But the ladder is shallow. At the ground-truth pose the truth is rank-1 in 37.5%, top-5 in 47.5%, top-25 in 66.2% and top-400 in 85.0%; in 15.0% of pairs it is not a competitive local maximum at all. Therefore a machine with BOTH perfect pose AND a perfect oracle re-ranker over 400 candidates scores 85% = 34/40. 40/40 is unreachable ON THIS CORPUS (see scope_correction). The buildable increment is rank-1 -> top-5, worth at most +10 pp, and it requires flawless discrimination among near-identical lattice translates using features measured at AUC 0.38-0.62 -- which is precisely what ten prior families failed to achieve.",
 "ceiling_ladder": {
  "ship_today_pts40": 13,
  "perfect_top5_reranker_pts40": 19,
  "perfect_top25_reranker_pts40": 26.5,
  "perfect_400_reranker_gt_pose_pts40": 34,
  "required_for_full_marks_pts40": 40
 },
 "caveats": [
  "n=80. 'Absent' means not among the top 400 NMS'd peaks, which folds together a weak local maximum and the 400 cap; it is a bound on re-ranking, not a claim that the site is undetectable at any radius.",
  "NMS radius is tied to estimate_lattice, so a bad pitch estimate can merge or split sites.",
  "p2_val only; no organizer data used."
 ],
